Subject: ksmserver -- launch the desktop directly (twin/kicker/kdesktop)

On substrate tdelauncher never processes incoming DCOP method calls (its DCOP
socket notifier does not fire in the TQt event loop, although it is registered),
so ksmserver's session bring-up silently stalls: startApplication() sends
DCOPRef("tdelauncher").send("exec_blind", ...) which is never delivered, and the
DCOP-driven autostart phases (DCOPRef(launcher).send("autoStart", N) plus the
autoStartNDone() signals) never run.  The result is a bare kdesktop background
with no window manager and no panel.

Until DCOP incoming-method delivery is fixed, launch session apps directly:
 - startApplication() forks+execvp()s the command (binaries are on $PATH under
   /opt/trinity/bin), in addition to the original (undelivered) exec_blind;
 - restoreSession()/startDefaultSession() also start the core desktop shell
   (kdesktop) and panel (kicker) directly, since the autostart phases that would
   normally do so never fire.

This is exactly what the working direct-launch starttde-substrate session does;
it makes the upstream /opt/trinity/bin/starttde bring up a full TDE desktop.
--- a/ksmserver/server.cpp
+++ b/ksmserver/server.cpp
@@ -121,6 +121,28 @@
     TQValueList<TQCString> argList;
     for ( int i=1; i < n; i++)
        argList.append( TQCString(command[i].latin1()));
+
+    /* substrate: ksmserver normally launches session apps (the window manager,
+     * panel, desktop, ...) by an async DCOP exec_blind() to tdelauncher.  On
+     * substrate tdelauncher never processes incoming DCOP method calls — its
+     * DCOP socket notifier does not fire in the TQt event loop even though it
+     * is registered — so exec_blind is silently dropped and twin/kicker/
+     * kdesktop never start (the desktop hangs at a bare kdesktop background).
+     * Until that DCOP-delivery issue is fixed, launch directly with
+     * fork()+execvp(): the binaries are on $PATH (/opt/trinity/bin), exactly
+     * as the working direct-launch starttde-substrate session does it. */
+    pid_t pid = fork();
+    if ( pid == 0 ) {
+        setsid();
+        char **argv = (char **) malloc( ( n + 1 ) * sizeof( char * ) );
+        argv[0] = strdup( app.data() );
+        for ( int i = 1; i < n; i++ )
+            argv[i] = strdup( command[i].latin1() );
+        argv[n] = 0;
+        execvp( argv[0], argv );
+        _exit( 127 );
+    }
+    /* keep the original DCOP path too, harmless if undelivered */
     DCOPRef( launcher ).send( "exec_blind", app, DCOPArg( argList, "TQValueList<TQCString>" ) );
 }
 
--- a/ksmserver/startup.cpp
+++ b/ksmserver/startup.cpp
@@ -143,6 +143,15 @@
         for (uint i = 0; i < wmCommands.count(); i++) {
             startApplication( wmCommands[i] );
         }
+        /* substrate: the autostart phases that normally bring up the desktop
+         * shell (kdesktop) and panel (kicker) are driven by DCOP round-trips to
+         * tdelauncher (DCOPRef(launcher).send("autoStart", N) and the
+         * autoStartNDone() signals), but tdelauncher never processes incoming
+         * DCOP method calls here, so those phases never run.  startApplication()
+         * now launches directly (see server.cpp), so launch the core desktop
+         * components ourselves to get a usable session. */
+        startApplication( TQStringList() << TQString::fromLatin1("kdesktop") );
+        startApplication( TQStringList() << TQString::fromLatin1("kicker") );
         if ((showFancyLogin) && (!startupNotifierIPDlg)) {
             startupNotifierIPDlg = KSMStartupIPDlg::showStartupIP();
         }
@@ -190,6 +199,10 @@
     else {
         startApplication( wm );
     }
+    /* substrate: bring up the desktop shell + panel directly — see the matching
